\chapter{Novostone and Firestone HIR integrations}
\label{ch:76}
\index{Novostone and Firestone HIR integrations}

The optional Novostone pass folds a bounded subset of closed checked expressions. The Firestone HIR pass supplies checked full-width integer folds and a divide-by-one rewrite within its documented subset. Each has an explicit CMake option and a named compiler optimization selector. These passes run before authored HIR rewrites. They work through checked adapters so the application needs no LibStone analysis runtime.

\section{Preserve traps and signed width}
Set up one build for each provider, record the selected option, and run equivalent source with the pass enabled and disabled. Use a successful full-width fold and an edge case that must trap or remain unoptimized. For division, check the boundary between a valid result and an invalid division before expecting constant replacement. Inspect generated artifacts and actual results. Firestone pattern compilation and the Novostone relational MIR analysis are separate capabilities, with distinct commands and phase boundaries in \cref{ch:20} and \cref{ch:86}.

\section{Find the first ineligible intermediate}
Evaluate the closed expression $(2147483647 + 1) - 1$ on paper. The
On1x signed 64-bit result is 2147483647, and that result fits in a
signed 32-bit field. The intermediate 2147483648 does not. Novostone's
checked adapter cannot project the whole tree to its wrapping 32-bit
dialect just because the final value fits. Firestone's signed 64-bit
arithmetic profile has room for both steps. Consequently a comparison
of fold counts across these providers needs the expression tree and
its intermediate ranges, not just the printed answer.

For a case in which even 64 bits do not settle the question, consider
the remainder of the least signed 64-bit value by negative one. The
mathematical remainder is zero and the Firestone evaluator returns zero,
but the On1x checked operation reports invalid modulo. The independent
On1x check prevents replacement by a successful literal. Put a print
before the expression and the regression criterion becomes quite precise:
the marker appears, then the program faults; it must not print a zero
and continue. An effect-free expression can still have an observable trap.

Finally inspect the handoff between passes. In the Firestone fixture,
a dynamic parameter is added to the remainder of five billion by two.
With the authored add-zero rule alone, the right child is not syntactically
zero. Enabling the earlier full-width fold exposes a literal zero and the
authored rule records a hit. Attribute the first replacement to Firestone
and the removal of the outer addition to the later rule; the same final
code reflects two distinct decisions.

